A||N||4||contra||How can I improve my gas mileage?||||1||
Z||000000||contra||08-07-2000||07:07 PM||||I think something is seriously wrong with my 87 GT's gas mileage....it's really poor. I have a cracked manifold which i'm hoping will get fixed next week, and i'm not sure if that affects my mileage. I have a K&N air filter that is clean, accell supercoil, and a 160degrees thermostat. I'm wondering if i should put back my 195degrees thermostat. Couple of other things are, i have no EGR, (it's completely disconnected), and no cat either. I know the EGR could screw alot of stuff up with the gas mileage, but it seemed fine for the longest time until recently. I've noticed the manifold crack is getting worse too, also my accelleration is getting sluggish. Would it be something as simple as a fuel filter needing to be changed?<BR>any suggestions?<BR>i'm hoping it's nothing expensive...this new paint job and manifold fix is costing me quite a bit.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||08-07-2000||08:11 PM||||Unless you have a performance chip, the 160 thermostat is causing at least some of your poor economy. At 160, the ECM is still thinking the engine is warming up and running rich.||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||08-07-2000||08:26 PM||||Yeah, 160 T-stat will do it, also check inflation pressures and alignment...if you have wide tires and rims, low air and/or poor inflation is a killer.<P>Gas mileage seems to be best between 55-85 degrees...if you've been in a heatwave, this will also affect mileage...high temps kill mileage just like cold weather does. The high temps increase evaporation of fuel and cars compensate by running rich.||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||08-07-2000||09:48 PM||||The cracked exhaust manifold is another problem. Air enters the exhaust stream through the cracks and the oxygen sensor is reading this oxygen as a lean condition. The computer tries to compensate for that lean condition by richening the fuel mixture causing your bad gas mileage and sluggish acceleration. Fix the exhaust leak and you will notice an improvement. The 160 degree thermostat does not help either but worst of all that thermostat will over time cause your motor to sludge up because the motor never warms up enough to burn off the combustion blow-by gases. It will also cause the fuel to condense on the cylinder walls washing the oil off them and accelerating the wear on your piston rings. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||contra||08-08-2000||01:46 AM||||ok great<BR>i'm going to put back my 195' thermostat<BR>it was only like $10 canadian for the 160' one so it's not like it was too much of a loss, i was just experimenting<BR>now if only the exhaust manifold would cost $10 to repair...i'm being quoted something around $300+ canadian in just labour alone because the welding is really easy. i also have a replacement manifold off a parts car fiero i might use instead too.||||reg||1
